<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges Bizet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1875">1875</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexandre César Léopold Bizet, Bizet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges Bizet was a French composer of the Romantic era, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1838, whose final opera, Carmen, became one of the most frequently performed works in the opera repertoire. A student at the Conservatoire de Paris, he won the Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1857, but the Paris opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> preferred the established repertoire, and he lived mainly by arranging the music of others. Neither Les pêcheurs de perles nor La jolie fille de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> was an immediate success, though his suite from the music for Alphonse Daudet's L'Arlésienne was. Carmen opened on 3 March 1875; Bizet, convinced it had failed, died of a heart attack three months later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-bizet.jpg" alt="Georges Bizet" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Étienne Carjat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georges_bizet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bizet was the only child of Adolphe Bizet, a former hairdresser and wigmaker who became a singing teacher, and Aimée Delsarte, an accomplished pianist from a musical family. He learned musical notation from his mother and by listening at the door of his father's classes. His parents thought him ready for the Conservatoire de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> at nine, a year under the minimum age, and the horn virtuoso Joseph Meifred waived the rule after hearing him. At the Villa Medici in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which he reached on 27 January 1858, he wrote the opera buffa Don Procopio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Bizet found the Opéra and the Opéra-Comique wedded to a traditional repertoire; only eight of the 54 Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> laureates between 1830 and 1860 had had works staged at the Opéra. He attended the Paris première of Wagner's Tannhäuser on 13 March 1861 and afterwards called Wagner above all living composers, which brought accusations of Wagnerism. He married Geneviève Halévy on 3 June 1869; their only child, Jacques, was born on 10 July 1872.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> he joined the National Guard and refused to leave besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His one-act opera Djamileh opened in May 1872 and closed after 11 performances. The music for Alphonse Daudet's L'Arlésienne was dismissed by critics on 1 October 1872, but the four-movement suite he drew from it was received with enthusiasm on 10 November under Pasdeloup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bizet suffered recurrent throat trouble, and in late May 1875 he swam in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> at Bougival. High fever and an apparent heart attack followed, and he died in the early hours of 3 June, his wedding anniversary, aged 36. Rumours of suicide arose, but physicians gave the cause as a cardiac complication of acute articular rheumatism.</p><p>After his death many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were lost and his works were revised by other hands; Carmen itself was turned into grand opera with recitatives by Guiraud. Carmen, after a lukewarm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> run of 45 performances, became a worldwide success after performances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1875 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1878. Les pêcheurs de perles was sung at the Metropolitan Opera on 13 November 1916 with Caruso, and the Symphony in C entered the repertory after its first performance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georges Bizet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
